        content:
          'Japan has four distinct seasons, and unlike most other destinations in this training, the season itself is often the primary reason clients book: spring (late Mar–early Apr) brings cherry blossom (sakura) season, an extremely popular but short and date-sensitive window — exact blossom timing varies year to year by 1–2 weeks and cannot be guaranteed months in advance, so set this expectation clearly rather than promising peak bloom on a fixed date booked far ahead. Autumn (Nov) brings equally spectacular koyo (autumn foliage), a slightly more predictable and increasingly popular alternative to spring. Summer (Jun–Aug) is hot, humid, and includes a rainy season (tsuyu, roughly Jun–early Jul) — the least recommended window for first-time visitors. Winter (Dec–Feb) is cold but clear, good for Mount Fuji visibility and, for adventure-focused clients, Japan\'s renowned ski resorts in Hokkaido/Nagano.',
